---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import SplitSection from '../components/SplitSection.astro';

const conditions = [
  {
    index: '01',
    title: 'An operator survives its own extraction',
    text: 'A step taken out of a successful run still works when the surrounding task changes. If it does not, what was extracted was a transcript, not an operator.',
  },
  {
    index: '02',
    title: 'Long-horizon state is cheap',
    text: 'Work that runs for weeks needs a runtime where context is a maintained object — not a longer prompt, and not a summary regenerated at the start of every session.',
  },
  {
    index: '03',
    title: 'Specialized models are economical',
    text: 'If every operation requires a frontier model, the economics never close. Rym exists to test whether compact models can carry the frequent operations.',
  },
  {
    index: '04',
    title: 'Others can build on it',
    text: 'An abstraction only its authors can use is a product feature. Infrastructure is what other people can build on without asking permission.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Vision"
  description="CHORD’s long-term vision: build the CUDA of the AI era — an abstraction layer where agent capabilities become composable, generalizable operations."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Company / 02"
    title="Vision"
    lede="Build the CUDA of the AI era. Stated as an ambition, not as a status report."
  />

  <div class="shell">
    <div class="credo">
      <p class="credo__line">The CUDA of the AI era.</p>
      <div class="credo__meta">
        <span class="label">Long-term vision</span>
        <p class="mono-note">
          CHORD has not achieved this. The phrase describes direction, and it is used here
          with the distance intact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <SplitSection label="The analogy" title="What the phrase actually means">
      <p>
        CUDA did one thing well: it turned a specific piece of hardware into a general
        programming surface. Before it, using a GPU meant shaping your problem around the
        machine’s peculiarities. After it, a large class of work became expressible,
        portable, and fast — not because the hardware changed, but because the abstraction
        did.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the analogy, and it is only an analogy. We have not built anything
        equivalent. What it describes is the thing we are aiming at: a layer where agent
        capabilities become composable operations with predictable behaviour, so that what
        works in one place can be used somewhere else without being rebuilt from scratch.
      </p>
    </SplitSection>

    <SplitSection label="The gap" title="Why the abstraction is missing">
      <p>
        Agent work today is largely bespoke. A team gets a result by arranging prompts,
        tools, context, and retries in a particular order for a particular task. The
        arrangement works. It is also difficult to name, difficult to test, and nearly
        impossible to reuse — the knowledge lives in the trajectory, not in an interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every capability wave eventually needs a programming model, because that is how work
        stops being a performance by its author and starts being something a system can
        repeat. Agents do not have one yet. We are working in that gap.
      </p>
    </SplitSection>

    <SplitSection label="Conditions" title="What would have to be true">
      <ol class="conditions" role="list">
        {
          conditions.map((item) => (
            <li class="condition">
              <span class="condition__index">{item.index}</span>
              <div>
                <h3 class="condition__title">{item.title}</h3>
                <p class="condition__text">{item.text}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </SplitSection>

    <SplitSection label="Position" title="Where CHORD is now">
      <p>
        Pointing at a target is not the same as arriving at it. CHORD today has a platform,
        a framework, a research direction, and an unopened community. The vision is the
        reason those four things exist in this order — not evidence that the vision has
        been reached.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the four conditions above turn out to be achievable, the result is not a better
        agent. It is a different relationship between a capability and the people who need
        it.
      </p>
    </SplitSection>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .credo {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 8fr) minmax(0, 4fr);
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    align-items: end;
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem) clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
  }

  .credo__line {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5.5rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 0.94;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
  }

  .credo__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .credo__meta .mono-note {
    max-width: 34ch;
  }

  .conditions {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .condition {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
    border-bottom: var(--hairline);
  }

  .condition:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  .condition__index {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--terracotta);
    padding-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .condition__title {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .condition__text {
    font-size: 0.9063rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 48ch;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .credo {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
